# Line endings, normalised once so they stop being a source of bugs.
#
# Git already stores LF in the index. Without an explicit rule, a Windows
# checkout materialises CRLF in the working tree, and anything that matches a
# pattern across a newline then sees something different from what CI sees.
# That has now cost this project twice:
#
#   - The CSP script hashes, computed over a CRLF working tree, passed locally
#     and would have served a blank site in production, because Render serves
#     the LF bytes git stored. csp.test.js still normalises for that reason.
#   - test/controls.test.js matches a two-line CSS selector. It passed on
#     Linux CI from the day it was written and failed on every Windows clone
#     -- the worst shape a failure can take, since it is invisible to the
#     people able to fix it and invisible to the people it inconveniences.
#
# eol=lf makes every checkout match the index and match CI.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Vendored third-party assets are kept byte-identical to their upstream copies,
# so they must not be line-ending normalized on checkout. This has to come
# after the rule above, because the last matching pattern wins.
vendor/** -text

# Git hooks are executed by sh. With core.autocrlf=true (the default on
# Windows) they would otherwise be checked out with CRLF endings, and sh fails
# on the shebang with "bad interpreter". Covered by the global rule now, but
# kept explicit: this one breaks the commit path itself, so it should not
# depend on a line further up staying the way it is.
.githooks/** text eol=lf

# Binary, so git leaves the bytes alone rather than guessing.
*.woff2 binary
*.woff  binary
*.ttf   binary
*.eot   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.webp  binary
